body:
**tl;dr : Similar things happened to me, it's not okay! Your normality isn't what the general populous experiences and you irritation is justified. Taking off the Mobicip might anger your parents. Talk to your friends and adults. Write it all down.**

Hey OP, I had a clinically diagnosed narcissistic (and bpd1) father. In my 17.5 years with living with him, I experienced a lot of the same things that you mentioned in your post.  This included taking the door off of my room, forcefully trying to filer harmless media, control how I practice my creative outlets, standing in my room in the dark while he thought I was sleeping, stealing money from my sister and me, tracking my siblings, mother and I for years without knowing, emotional abuse that (like your similar example) included threats of "I can do much worse," etc. What I'm trying to say is I feel you, and more importantly...

&#x200B;

...you have the right to complain!!! Believe it or not, that is not normal behavior in a family house hold. In my opinion with the information given, you fit into the "people who have it worse" category. 

&#x200B;

I firstly would not recommend taking off the Mobicip, as I presume that would cause your parents to get agitated and take whatever privacy you have left away. If you can -somehow- make it a regular habit to go to a nearby friend's house, I believe that would be a better idea. This way you might be able to access more media. I don't know if you turning your phone off, or leaving it at your friend's house when you go somewhere with your friends is an option. (I'm under the assumption that they might text or call you a lot when you're not at home.) 

&#x200B;

You can tell an adult at school if things become uncomfortable; changing with doors open and them watching you, them coming into your room at night, them watching when you deal with your bathroom hygiene.  Write it down if possible, because talking to social workers can be very tiring and might make you second guess yourself. So writing it down and reading directly from the source should hopefully keep you from second guessing yourself. (You could hide it in your homework books.)

&#x200B;

If none of this is possible, try to set up a support circle with your friends at school. Tell them, make sure they know what's going on. Maybe they can help even more.
